deep.reconstruction:
The page is a printed 18th-century German arithmetic textbook (sheet signature 'A 3', p. 5). It is not by Gauss. The only handwritten matter is a strip at the left edge. That strip is the cut-off right margin of the facing manuscript page, i.e. the previous folio.

(A) Printed text.
- §3 conclusion: arithmetic must teach three things: (1) expressing magnitude as number; (2) the operations on numbers, three increasing and three decreasing; (3) finding the unknown from the known by comparison (equations).
- §4 'arbitrary propositions' (conventions) of decimal place-value notation:
  1) The digits 1-9 form the class of units.
  2) A digit in the k-th place from the right is worth d·10^(k-1). Empty places are written 0. Each move one place left multiplies the value by 10 (3000 = 10·300, 300 = 10·30).
  3) Ten units of a lower class make one unit of the next class.
  4) Naming proceeds in triads (Einer, Zehner, Hunderte), using the long scale:
     - places 4-6: tausend (10^3);
     - places 7-9: Millionen (10^6);
     - places 10-12: tausend Millionen (10^9);
     - places 13-15: Billionen (10^12);
     - places 16-18: tausend Billionen (10^15);
     - then Trillionen (10^18).
  Marking off groups of three digits from the right is called 'Numeriren'.
- Worked example: 3|467''|891|076'|384|602 = 3467891076384602. Marks: '' at 10^12 (Billionen), ' at 10^6 (Millionen). The next page reads it as 3467·10^12 + 891076·10^6 + 384602.

(B) Handwritten marginal fragments (Gauss). The visible word stubs are '..gebr. fu..', 'Reihe', 'Wurz..', 'X so da..', 'Man so..', 'f(x,α)', '...n dα^n', 'n-1', '-h-αξ=0', 'dα^n', '(x-h)^n d^n'. They match line-by-line the previous page's text:
- the heading 'Die Anzahl aller verschiedenen algebr. functionen';
- the problem 'φ(x,α)=0, man soll f(x,α) in eine Reihe verwandeln', with root x=h of φ(x,0) and φ(x,0)=(x-h)·X;
- the coefficient formula p^(n) = (1/n!) d^n f/dα^n − (1/(n−1)!) d^(n−1)/dx^(n−1){(x−h)^n (1/n!) ∂^n[f'(x) log φ(x,α)]/∂α^n};
- the substitution x − h − αξ = 0.
This is a Lagrange-Bürmann-type series for a function of an implicitly defined root. No new derivation appears on this page itself.
